<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!-- Peace Garden's privacy manifest.

	     **Three of the four arrays are empty, and that is the whole design
	     rather than an omission.** A plant is a seed plus a birthday, derived
	     on the phone that holds it; there is no server, no account and no sync,
	     and the app makes no network request of its own. docs/PHASES.md is
	     explicit that anything leaving the phone is deliberately unbuilt.

	     The one thing that does leave a phone is a seed, a name and two nonces,
	     handed directly to one other phone during a meeting. That is
	     device-to-device over Multipeer and reaches nobody else — not the
	     developer, not a partner, not a host. Under Apple's definition
	     "collect" means transmitting off device in a way that lets you or a
	     partner access it, and nobody here can. So `NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes`
	     is empty, and the App Store Connect answer is Data Not Collected.

	     `identifierForVendor` is read once, in `SeedMint`, and hashed into a
	     seed. It is never transmitted as an identifier and never used to track
	     anybody. It is the one line a reviewer might ask about, so it is named
	     here rather than left to be discovered. -->

	<key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
	<false/>
	<key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
	<array/>
	<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
	<array/>

	<!-- Required-reason APIs. **One category, and it used to be two.**

	     `ProcessInfo.systemUptime` was an entropy ingredient in `SeedMint` and
	     would have obliged a `SystemBootTime` declaration whose every published
	     reason string is worded for measuring elapsed time between events in
	     the app. None described what it was doing. It was dropped on
	     6 September 2026 rather than declared under a reason that did not fit —
	     see the note on `SeedMint`. -->
	<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<!-- Eleven `@AppStorage` properties in SettingsView, more in
			     PlantStageView, Places and Sharing. All of it is this app's own
			     preferences, read and written by this app alone, which is
			     exactly what CA92.1 describes. -->
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryUserDefaults</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>CA92.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
